virglrenderer: Venus refuses what reached vkr's asserts

What: the assert()s a guest's commands reached (virglrenderer is built with
assertions on) now end only the app's Venus context, with a log line:
- vkImportSemaphoreResourceMESA with a resource id other than 0;
- one object id twice in one call (vkAllocateCommandBuffers,
  vkAllocateDescriptorSets, vkCreate*Pipelines), checked on a sorted copy of the
  ids before any driver call;
- host-visible memory the guest asked to export as OPAQUE_FD (the emulated
  KHR_external_memory_fd) that has a Metal heap exports the heap;
- vkCreateRingMESA that ends the context after listing the ring (a monitor
  period of 0): a ring that never started is not stopped at the context's end,
  by vkDestroyRingMESA or at the end of its resource. A ring whose thread was not
  made ends the context, and the ring monitor counts as started only once its
  thread runs (these two only fix the asserts: a guest that runs the process out
  of threads still ends QEMU elsewhere, #624).
The other 73 asserts in src/venus are internal (constants, host values, or
checked before); they stay.
Why: each ended QEMU's process (SIGABRT): resource id 5 (vkr_queue.c), the same
command buffer id twice (vkr_context.h), an OPAQUE_FD export on KosmicKrisp
(vkr_device_memory.c), a ring monitor period of 0 and then the app's end
(vkr_ring.c), on KosmicKrisp and MoltenVK alike but for the export (M4, macOS
27). Two rings creating or destroying one id at once stay open (#624).
Upstream: the semaphore, the ids and the ring are vkr bugs on every host (draft
with the missing entries'); the export choice is OmacVM's (Metal heaps).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-robust.c: a-sem-import-res, a-dup-alloc and
a-monitor0 (then the context's end) end the process before the patch and only
the context after it; x-opaque exports on KosmicKrisp; two command buffers in one
call, a ring and its context's end, DMA_BUF exports still work.

diff --git a/src/venus/vkr_common.c b/src/venus/vkr_common.c
index 34f8ced..ae7e2be 100644
--- a/src/venus/vkr_common.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_common.c
@@ -273,6 +273,46 @@ object_array_fini(struct object_array *arr)
    free(arr->handle_storage);
 }
 
+static int
+object_id_compare(const void *a, const void *b)
+{
+   const vkr_object_id x = *(const vkr_object_id *)a, y = *(const vkr_object_id *)b;
+   return x < y ? -1 : x > y;
+}
+
+/* OmacVM (virgl-venus-guest-asserts.patch): the guest names the new objects' ids, and one
+ * id twice in one call would put two objects under it (vkr_context_add_object asserts
+ * that it never happens). Sorted, so a guest's count costs n log n. */
+static bool
+object_array_ids_unique(struct vkr_context *ctx,
+                        uint32_t count,
+                        VkObjectType obj_type,
+                        size_t handle_size,
+                        const void *obj_id_handles)
+{
+   if (count < 2)
+      return true;
+   vkr_object_id *ids = malloc(sizeof(*ids) * count);
+   if (!ids)
+      return false;
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+      const void *obj_id_handle = (const char *)obj_id_handles + handle_size * i;
+      ids[i] = vkr_cs_handle_load_id((const void **)obj_id_handle, obj_type);
+   }
+   qsort(ids, count, sizeof(*ids), object_id_compare);
+   for (uint32_t i = 1; i < count; i++) {
+      if (ids[i] == ids[i - 1]) {
+         vkr_context_refuse(ctx, true, "an object array",
+                            "%u new objects of type %d name id %" PRIu64 " twice", count,
+                            obj_type, ids[i]);
+         free(ids);
+         return false;
+      }
+   }
+   free(ids);
+   return true;
+}
+
 bool
 object_array_init(struct vkr_context *ctx,
                   struct object_array *arr,
@@ -282,6 +322,9 @@ object_array_init(struct vkr_context *ctx,
                   size_t handle_size,
                   const void *obj_id_handles)
 {
+   if (!object_array_ids_unique(ctx, count, obj_type, handle_size, obj_id_handles))
+      return false;
+
    arr->count = count;
 
    arr->objects = malloc(sizeof(*arr->objects) * count);
